# The Fleet Console — N Intelligences, one shell [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

> **The renderer was always plural; the data layer never was.** The singleton assumption does not live
> in the composition model: parameterised composites, `repeat`, widget-scoped variables and bindable
> layout order are all built and shipping, and eight composites are in the tree today. It lives in four
> Python sources that each assume ONE Intelligence, ONE session, ONE bridge, ONE transcript pin — and in
> one place worse than any of them, the harness `cwd` that keys a session's memory store. So this is not
> "make the console a fleet console"; it is **remove four singletons from the data layer, fix one scope
> lever, and author one composite.** That is a much smaller and better-bounded build than the framing
> suggests, and this document says so plainly so nobody budgets for a rewrite. Two things in it are
> higher-risk than the rest and are specced first: the `cwd=None` fork between the two spawn paths,
> which is writing one memory store from two live Intelligences RIGHT NOW; and the reconciliation of
> David's two ratified calls — memory PER ROLE and a dedicated per-Intelligence home — which conflict as
> literally stated because cwd can key only one thing. A build that ships N panes over a shared memory
> pile, or per-role memory without a derived index, has shipped the defect this spec exists to close.

- see-also: [CXC-FC-SHAPE], [CXC-FC-THREE-AXES], [CXC-FC-ACCEPT-INSTANCING], [CXC-FC-ACCEPT-MEMORY], [CXC-FC-ACCEPT-DATALAYER], [CXC-FC-ACCEPT-GUARDS]
- entry-point: [CXC-FC-SHAPE] is where a builder starts — it is the five-item change list, and it is deliberately shorter than the framing. [CXC-FC-THREE-AXES] is the model every other node resolves onto.
- acceptance-note: the four ACCEPT nodes above are this document's whole acceptance contract (FC-C1..FC-C20); a reader who wants the verdict rather than the design reads only those.

---

## 0. What this build IS

### [CXC-FC-THESIS] The renderer was always plural; the data layer never was

- premise: the plural half is DONE. `expand_components()` substitutes `{{param}}` at load time, type-preserving (`python/composeio/render/engine/components.py:248`, called once from `parser.py:1742`); eight composites ship in `python/composeio/render/examples/components/`; `repeat` unrolls N children at load; [CXC-BIND-VARS] supports widget-scoped variable blocks; [CAP-BINDABLE-ORDER] reorders and hides a live surface from a bus publish with 9 passing tests.
- key-insight: the singleton assumption therefore is not architectural — it is four literal call sites plus one defaulted keyword argument. Naming them individually is what converts a vague "fleet console" ambition into a bounded change list.
- measured-2026-08-12: `intelligence/console/data_sources.py::build_console_sources` appends exactly ONE `channel_bridge` (line 508) and exactly one each of `self_publisher` / `transcript_publisher` / `deliverable_monitor` (lines 484-495); `resolve_session_jsonl` (line 152) runs once at console boot for one session; there is no roster source; `python/composeio/render/engine/runtime.py:79` hardcodes `CONSOLE_BBOX_NS = "_app/console/bbox"`.
- consequence: the spec's own shape follows the finding. Section 3 is the composite (author it, add no engine code); sections 5 and 6 are the removals; section 4 is the one place where the fix is NOT a removal, because memory scoping requires a new invariant rather than the deletion of an old assumption.
- criticality: foundational
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-SHAPE] The change list, and what it is NOT

- requirement: the builder MUST treat this as five bounded changes, not a rewrite. (1) Move the chat/instance binding from a document-level `variables:` block to widget/param scope and author the `intelligence_pane` composite. (2) Give every Intelligence a ROLE-keyed project dir and de-fork the two spawn paths onto one seam. (3) Instantiate N channel bridges and N telemetry publisher sets from the declared instance list. (4) Add ONE roster source and re-namespace the app bbox topic. (5) Add the engage/configure toggle and de-scope the console config tab.
- not-in-scope: no new engine widget class; no parser change; no second console process; no change to the Terminal Widget's contract; no roster-driven pane discovery ([DD-FC-PANE-SOURCE]).
- sequencing: [PRINCIPLE-ONE-PASS] applies — this ships in ONE pass if the spec is complete. The ordering in [CXC-FC-ORDER] is the author's ordering and is explicitly NOT a delivery contract.
- depends-on: [CXC-FC-THESIS]
- see-also: [CXC-FC-VAR-SCOPE], [CXC-FC-DEFORK], [CXC-FC-S1-BRIDGES], [CXC-FC-S2-TELEMETRY], [CXC-FC-S3-ROSTER], [CXC-FC-S4-BBOX], [CXC-FC-PANE-MODES], [CXC-FC-CONFIG-TAB], [CXC-FC-OUT]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-BASELINE] The terminal is RECOVERY; both console surfaces are ONGOING

- premise: interacting with an Intelligence in a bare terminal (iTerm) is a recovery/fallback mode, not ongoing operations — [DEC-ICL-UNHARNESSED-RECOVERY-INSTANCE], which is why a recovery instance wears a self-describing `recovery-` prefix. [CXC-ICL-CLI-UNSUPPORTED] states the same negatively: the console's answer to a problem cannot be "open iTerm."
- consequence: (this is what drives the whole build) if the terminal is recovery-only then EVERY ongoing affordance must exist in the console. Today the conversation affordance exists exactly once, so a second Intelligence has nowhere to be talked to and the fallback silently absorbs the load. That is how a recovery path becomes the default without anyone deciding it should.
- requirement: two surfaces share this baseline and NEITHER may be specced as a backup. The CONVERSATION surface (bus-native chat) is the ongoing conversational path. The TERMINAL WIDGET (a real `claude` TUI inside a ComposeApp, [SPEC:CXC-TERM]) is the ongoing KEYBOARD path, for what only a TUI can express — startup dialogs, permission prompts, TUI interaction — and [CXC-TERM-SUPERSEDES-55] has it superseding the external-terminal spawn once glass-green.
- prohibition: specifying the Terminal Widget as a backup path is forbidden ([CXC-FC-ANTI]). A surface built as a fallback is under-built, and an under-built keyboard path keeps the console dependent on iTerm — which reinstates exactly the condition [CXC-ICL-CLI-UNSUPPORTED] rejects.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

---

## 1. The instancing unit

### [CXC-FC-THREE-AXES] ROLE, INSTANCE ID and HOST are three separate axes — this is the model the whole spec encodes

- definition: identity in the fleet has THREE independent axes, and every requirement in this document resolves onto exactly one of them. Conflating any two is the root of the defects section 4 removes.

| Axis | Determines | Example |
|---|---|---|
| ROLE | memory, bootstrap tier, agent registration | `composeux-architect` |
| INSTANCE ID | channel topics, telemetry, locks, voice | `architect` · `architect-hosted` · `recovery-architect-2` |
| HOST | where it renders | the Terminal Widget in the console · a bare terminal (recovery) |
- worked-example: `architect` (console-hosted), `architect-hosted` (widget-hosted) and `recovery-architect-2` (bare terminal) are THREE INSTANCES OF ONE ROLE sharing ONE memory store, with three distinct voices. Per-role memory ([DD-FC-MEMORY-GRAIN]) and the recovery path are the same mechanism seen from two directions.
- requirement: no mechanism may key one axis off another. Memory MUST NOT be keyed by instance id; a channel topic MUST NOT be keyed by role; nothing may be keyed by host at all, because where an Intelligence renders is the one axis that carries no identity.
- prohibition: `role` and `instance_id` MUST NOT be derived from each other by naming convention. `architect-hosted` does not mean "role architect" by string surgery — it means whatever its declaration says, and an undeclared role REFUSES ([CXC-FC-UNIT]).
- consequence: the composite declares axes 1 and 2 and is instantiated under axis 3, which is why the pane parameter list is `(instance_id, role)` and the pane's HOST is simply where the `{use: intelligence_pane, ...}` line sits.
- criticality: foundational
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-UNIT] One declared instance; every binding derived

- requirement: an Intelligence in the console is declared by a `(instance_id, role)` PAIR and nothing else — axes 1 and 2 of [CXC-FC-THREE-AXES]. Every bus topic, every daemon argument, every identity home and every config path MUST be derived from that pair at instantiation. No second per-instance state may be introduced anywhere in the scene, the data layer or the widget tree.
- default: `role` has NO safe default. `python/composeio/render/widgets/terminal.py:283` currently defaults `role: str = "architect"`, which was harmless while one Intelligence existed and becomes a silent mis-keying of the project dir under [CXC-FC-PROJECT-DIR]. The default MUST be removed and an absent role MUST refuse, not guess.
- change: the narrative's "one parameter: `instance_id`" is refined to a PAIR after checking the code the pane must drive.
- context_for_change:
  prior_state: "§3 of the design narrative states that everything derives from one parameter, `instance_id`, and lists `role -> manifest chain` as one of the derived bindings."
  reasoning: "`role` is not derivable from `instance_id`. `ptycore.py::resolve_meta_manifest(intelligence_id, role)` takes role as a PARAMETER, and the whole point of chain leg 3 ([CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN]) is that a fresh child MISSES leg 2, so there is no per-id manifest to read the role out of. `terminal.py:181` already records the ratified scheme as scene-DECLARED: the scene carries a `role` PLUS a distinct instance id."
  rejected_alternative: "Deriving role from a naming convention on the id (`architect-hosted` -> `architect`). Rejected: it is a second, undeclared identity mechanism that works until the first id that does not follow the convention, and it silently produces a WRONG project dir rather than a refusal."
  invalidating_assumption: "That the manifest chain still needs an externally supplied role. If leg 2 ever became mandatory (a curated manifest per instance) the role would be readable from the manifest and the pair would collapse back to a single parameter."
  implication: "The composite declares two params, not one; the refusal path at [CXC-FC-PROJECT-DIR] has something concrete to refuse ON; and [CXC-FC-BINDINGS] splits its table into id-derived and role-derived rows."
- criticality: foundational
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-BINDINGS] The derivation table — the whole per-instance surface, enumerated

- requirement: these are the ONLY bindings. A builder who needs a per-instance value not in this table MUST add a row here rather than thread a new parameter through the scene.

| Derived from | Binding | Value |
|---|---|---|
| `instance_id` | conversation topics | `intelligence/<id>/{out,in,compose,channel/alive}` |
| `instance_id` | telemetry namespace | `self/<id>/*` and `thread/<id>/*` (the `--namespace-by-id` form already passed at `data_sources.py:485`) |
| `instance_id` | channel bridge | `channel_bridge --id <id>`; singleton flock `channel-bridge-<id>` |
| `instance_id` | transcript pin | this instance's OWN `.jsonl`, re-resolvable ([CXC-FC-S2-TELEMETRY]) |
| `instance_id` | config | `intelligence/<id>/config/*` — ALREADY per-id today; it is the console TAB that assumes one ([CXC-FC-CONFIG-TAB]) |
| `instance_id` | composite id root | the `id_prefix` param, per `segment_bar`'s precedent ([CXC-FC-COMPOSITE]) |
| `role` | manifest chain | leg 3, `intelligence/meta/<role>.meta.json` ([CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN]) |
| `role` | project dir / cwd | `~/compose-instances/<role>/` — memory, transcripts and agent-def registration ([CXC-FC-PROJECT-DIR]) |
| `(id, role)` | spawn | the Terminal Widget's existing scene-declared attributes (`terminal.py:283`, `terminal.py:309`) |
- prohibition: no per-instance state outside this table. Each addition is a new thing that can be set inconsistently between two panes, and an inconsistency between panes is invisible on the glass by construction.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

---

## 2. The pane is a COMPOSITE — parameterised composites are already built

### [CXC-FC-COMPOSITE] The pane is authorable with ZERO engine code, and a widget class here is a spec violation

- requirement: the `intelligence_pane` MUST be authored as a `components:` composite instantiated with `{use: intelligence_pane, instance_id: ..., role: ...}`. Adding a rasterising widget class for the pane is FORBIDDEN — [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]'s composites-not-code-widgets rule applies in full, and here it is a violation rather than a shortcut because the mechanism the class would duplicate is already in the tree and already tested.
- evidence: verified in-tree 2026-08-12 -- `components:` declares reusable widget shapes; `{use: <name>, <params...>}` instantiates them; `expand_components(config)` (`components.py:248`) deep-copies the template, substitutes `{{param}}` type-preservingly and runs ONCE at parse time, so the widget factory never learns what a component is (COMP-4). Eight composites ship today — dropdown, keyboard, numpad, slider, switch, segment_bar and two progress bars.
- two-phase-split: exactly the two mechanisms a parameterised composite needs -- `{{param}}` is resolved at LOAD by the expander (pass-through from upstream) and `${ns}` is left untouched and resolved at RUNTIME (internal and bus traffic). That split is documented as COMP-3 at `components.py:17-19`.
- collision-precedent: multi-instance collision was ANTICIPATED AND SOLVED -- `segment_bar.compose.yaml:50` declares `params: [value_ns]` and line 52 defaults `id_prefix: seg` with the stated purpose "unique id root per instance (avoids id clash)"; every generated id is `{{id_prefix}}_...` (lines 68, 82). Call sites already pass namespaces as params — `{use: progress_bar, value_ns: demo/fuel_level, width: 246}`.
- consequence: the pane composite takes `instance_id`, `role` and an `id_prefix`, and passes namespaces down as `{{param}}` values exactly as `progress_bar` does today. There is no engine gap to fill.
- criticality: high
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-VAR-SCOPE] `chat_id` moves from document scope to widget/param scope

- premise: today's binding is the right mechanism at the wrong scope. `intelligence/console/console.compose.yaml:103-104` carries a DOCUMENT-level `variables: {chat_id: architect-hosted}`, substituted at parse time before `namespaces:` is read, and every channel site derives from `$chat_id` — the four topic declarations, the `chat_thread` namespaces, the `composer_field` and `link_health` namespaces, and the `--id` handed to the in-process `channel_bridge`.
- requirement: `chat_id` MUST become a composite PARAMETER (`instance_id`) resolved per pane by `expand_components`. The document-level `variables:` entry MUST be removed, not left beside the param — two live sources for one binding is the fork class this corpus keeps paying for.
- constraint: the headless fallback `start-console-daemons.sh` reads the SAME variable out of this SAME file (`console.compose.yaml:93`). That reader MUST be moved to the declared instance list in the same change, or the headless path silently keeps a one-Intelligence view of a fleet scene.
- verification: a scene declaring two panes expands to two complete sets of channel bindings with no id collision, and `grep -n 'variables:' intelligence/console/console.compose.yaml` shows no `chat_id`.
- dangling-reference-note: this node's SUPERSEDES edge targets [DEC-CONSOLE-CHAT-ID-PARAM], which exists ONLY as a code citation (`data_sources.py:413`, `console.compose.yaml:76`) and has NO node in the decision graph. The edge is authored anyway and will lint as dangling ON PURPOSE — that is preserve-and-flag doing its job, and it makes a decision recorded only in a comment VISIBLE instead of quietly absent. Filing it is the architect's call, not this spec's.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

---

## 3. Bootstrapping — three tiers, and only two are modelled today

### [CXC-FC-TIERS] PLATFORM / ROLE / INSTANCE, and the tier that is implicit

- definition: bootstrapping instructions divide into three tiers. David, 2026-08-12: there are agent-specific AND common bootstrapping instructions, and today only the bottom two are modelled.

| Tier | Contents | Home | Today |
|---|---|---|---|
| PLATFORM — every Intelligence | do not slice work to emulate human programming methods, pursue extended development cycles ([PRINCIPLE-ONE-PASS]); how to drive lts-engine (`bootstrap/toolbox.md`); the wake ritual (`bootstrap/session-bootstrap-read.md`); [SBR-TRUST] | `bootstrap/` | IMPLICIT — a directory everyone happens to read, not a declared layer |
| ROLE — every instance of this agent def | `.claude/agents/<role>.md`; role-specific dusk gifts; the role's minimum tool set | `<role>.meta.json` | exists (`intelligence/meta/composeux-architect.meta.json`) |
| INSTANCE — this one | memory; its handoff; channel id; transcript; config dials | `<id>.meta.json` | exists (`intelligence/meta/architect.meta.json`) |
- requirement: the PLATFORM tier MUST become EXPLICIT — a declared layer the manifest chain resolves, not a directory read by habit. The chain `META_MANIFEST -> <id> -> <role> -> REFUSE` (`ptycore.py::resolve_meta_manifest`) already composes the bottom two; the platform tier is added ABOVE the role leg and is inherited BY REFERENCE.
- rationale: a `composeio-hardware-architect` must inherit the shared guidance by reference instead of someone copying those sentences into a second role def where the copies drift — [INSIGHT-FORK-DIVERGENCE-IS-A-CLASS] applied to bootstrap prose. The cost of the implicit tier is invisible until the SECOND role exists, which is precisely when this build creates it.
- prohibition: the platform tier MUST NOT be implemented by copying platform text into each role manifest. A copy is a fork with a delay fuse.
- see-also: [CXC-FC-GIFT-TIERS], [CXC-FC-GIFT-MECHANISM]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-GIFT-TIERS] Dusk gifts are tiered, not flat

- requirement: each dusk gift MUST declare its tier rather than sit in a flat directory. The ComposeUX model problems are ROLE-tier — they belong to whoever holds the map. "The light from outside the self" ([PRINCIPLE-LIGHT-FROM-OUTSIDE-THE-SELF]) and the read-vs-reason discipline are PLATFORM-tier. The handoff is INSTANCE-tier.
- consequence: a new role inherits the platform gifts and NONE of the ComposeUX model problems, which is the whole point — a hardware architect reasoning through ComposeUX widget-model problems is being oriented to somebody else's map.
- verification: every file under `bootstrap/dusk-gifts/` resolves to exactly one declared tier, and a role manifest lists its role-tier gifts explicitly.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-GIFT-MECHANISM] The withheld answer is withheld BY THE FILESYSTEM — already landed, and the guard is what remains

- premise: the dusk gifts' withheld-answer mechanism used `<details>` blocks, which is a BROWSER affordance. `Read` returns the whole file, answers included, so an agent cannot un-read them and "reason first" was an honour system the tool defeats. It failed for two consecutive instances and was found independently by the architect AND by the hosted child — strong corroboration.
- measured-2026-08-12: the split has ALREADY LANDED. Commit `4bca26e` ("the withheld answer becomes withheld BY MECHANISM, one file per problem") created `bootstrap/dusk-gifts/answers/` (18 files, P1..P11 with b/c/d variants) and `bootstrap/dusk-gifts/answers-render-engineer/` (R1..R3). The three remaining `<details>` occurrences — `README.md:32`, `registration-protocol.md:52-53` — are PROSE DESCRIBING the retired mechanism, not live withholding.
- requirement: what remains in this build's scope is therefore NOT the split but the GUARD and the tier assignment. A test MUST assert that no file under `bootstrap/dusk-gifts/` outside `answers*/` contains an answer inside a `<details>` block, so the mechanism cannot silently regress the next time a gift is authored.
- change: the narrative scoped the filesystem split into this build; measurement moved it to DONE and narrowed the remaining requirement to a regression guard plus [CXC-FC-GIFT-TIERS].
- context_for_change:
  prior_state: "§5 of the design narrative lists the `<details>` fix as a platform-tier bootstrap change belonging in this build's scope."
  reasoning: "The narrative was written 2026-08-12 and the fix landed the same day at 11:51. Specifying work that is already done wastes a builder's pass and, worse, invites a second implementation of it beside the first."
  rejected_alternative: "Restating the split as a requirement anyway, on the grounds that a spec should describe the target state. Rejected: this spec is a work order for one build, and an already-satisfied requirement with no guard reads identically to an unimplemented one."
  invalidating_assumption: "That commit 4bca26e is the whole fix. If a gift store exists outside `bootstrap/dusk-gifts/` — a role-tier gift set under `.claude/agents/`, for instance — the split has not been applied there and the requirement reopens for that store."
  implication: "Acceptance criterion FC-C17 tests the GUARD and the tier assignment, not the split."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

