## 4. Memory scoping — the highest-risk content in this build

### [CXC-FC-MEMORY-REQUIRED] Memory MUST be scoped per Intelligence, and today it is not — this is live

- requirement: David, 2026-08-12 — "local claude memory MUST be unique per intelligence/agent def'n." The scope UNIT is the ROLE ([DD-FC-MEMORY-GRAIN]) and "role" means EVERY role in the roster, not only top-level Intelligences ([CXC-FC-ROLE-MEMORY]); the requirement is that memory is never scoped to the REPO.
- measured-2026-08-12: (measured, not inferred) `python/composeio/ptycore.py:233` carries the comment `cwd: None -> the host's cwd -> THE REPO`. `ptycore.py::project_dir_key` (line 393) documents the consequence exactly — "distinct cwds -> distinct project keys -> transcripts + memory that never co-locate; a SHARED cwd collapses N instances into one key." The child spawned by the Terminal Widget on 2026-08-12 inherited the repo as cwd, so its transcript landed under `~/.claude/projects/-Users-<user>-Dropbox-Maker-composeio/` — the SAME project key as its host. There is one memory store for that key. Host and child are writing the same `MEMORY.md` right now.
- consequence: the defect is not "two instances share a store". It is that the store is keyed by THE REPO, so every Intelligence that ever runs with the repo as cwd — any role, any purpose — lands in one pile. Fixing the grain to ROLE ([CXC-FC-PROJECT-DIR]) is what makes the sharing deliberate and bounded instead of accidental and unbounded.
- criticality: foundational
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-ROLE-MEMORY] "Role" means the WHOLE agent roster; store names are DERIVED, never chosen

- requirement: EVERY role in the roster gets a memory store, not only top-level Intelligences. `composeux-render-engineer`, `composeio-expert`, `git-expert` and `defect-investigator` each get one, exactly as `composeux-architect` does.
- rationale: today every dispatch starts COLD, which is why briefs must re-state hazards those same roles already discovered. Role memory gives a dispatched role continuity ACROSS dispatches, and briefs shrink as the store grows. This is the payoff that makes per-role the right grain rather than merely the cheaper one.
- requirement: store names are DERIVED, never chosen. A role's name is ratified as the BASENAME OF ITS ROLE DESCRIPTOR ([CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN]'s same rule), so the store set is computed from `.claude/agents/*.md` plus the FactLattice agent pool. Measured 2026-08-12: 6 descriptors in `.claude/agents/` and 12 in `factlattice-deployment/agents/`.
- default: a store is created LAZILY on first write. A role that has never written a fact has no directory, so an empty store and a never-used role are the same observable state and neither needs provisioning.
- prohibition: no hand-maintained list of stores. A list is a second source of truth beside the descriptor set, and it goes stale the first time a role is added — which is the fork class [INSIGHT-FORK-DIVERGENCE-IS-A-CLASS] names.
- verification: a test asserts the derived store-name set equals the descriptor basenames on disk, so adding a role descriptor is the ONLY act needed to give that role a store.
- criticality: high
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-STORE-ACCESS] ONE store, TWO access paths — and cwd CANNOT be the mechanism for a dispatched role

- premise: ⚠ cwd is the lever for a TOP-LEVEL Intelligence and is UNAVAILABLE for a dispatched subagent. Dispatched agents run in worktrees under `~/.cache/claude-worktrees/…` — a DIFFERENT cwd, and an EPHEMERAL one. Role memory keyed by cwd would materialise INSIDE a worktree and be destroyed by a routine prune.
- evidence: this is [INSIGHT-WORKTREE-IS-NOT-THE-ENVIRONMENT] instance (4) DURABILITY applied to memory — full success reported, deliverable silently gone. That instance is recorded as having NO structural fix, so the hazard is live, not hypothetical, and a cwd-keyed design would walk straight into it.
- requirement: there is exactly ONE store per role, at `~/compose-instances/<role>/memory/`, outside every worktree and therefore prune-proof. It is reached by TWO ACCESS PATHS: a top-level Intelligence loads it AUTOMATICALLY because its cwd IS the role home; a dispatched subagent addresses it BY PATH, stated in its role descriptor, and reads/writes it with ordinary file tools.
- prohibition: this MUST be built as ONE store. Same bytes, same location; only the LOADING MECHANISM differs by caller. A builder who reads "two access paths" as "two memory systems" has built the fork this rule exists to prevent — and the two would diverge invisibly, because each caller would only ever see its own.
- verification: a test asserts a subagent-written fact is visible to the top-level Intelligence of the same role WITHOUT any sync step, and that the path named in the role descriptor resolves to the same directory the top-level load resolves to. One assertion, both directions, or the fork is untested.
- criticality: foundational
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-RECOVERY-SAME-ROLE] Recovery instantiates the ACTUAL role — there is no surrogate mechanism to build

- requirement: the emergency case needs NO cross-role access mechanism. David, 2026-08-12: "in an emergency, we should instantiate composeux-architect OUTSIDE the console widget." Recovery instantiates the ACTUAL ROLE, merely hosted elsewhere — same role descriptor, same manifest chain, same role home, therefore THE SAME MEMORY by the ordinary mechanism.
- prohibition: no cross-role read, no borrowing, no surrogate-write attribution invariant. Nothing is standing in for anything, so there is nothing to attribute. A design in which one role reads another's store is FORBIDDEN — it would be solving a problem this reframe removes, and it would introduce an access rule that has to be got right forever.
- consequence: this composes with [DEC-ICL-UNHARNESSED-RECOVERY-INSTANCE] rather than reopening it. The `recovery-` prefix keeps a recovery instance from ever claiming the canonical identity on axis 2 (INSTANCE ID) while it shares axis 1 (ROLE) completely — which is exactly the separation [CXC-FC-THREE-AXES] exists to make expressible.
- worked-example: live as this spec was written -- the architect authoring the source narrative was `composeux-architect` running in iTerm, outside the console — precisely this recovery mode, and precisely why [CXC-FC-BASELINE] matters. Under the fix its cwd is `~/compose-instances/composeux-architect/` and it holds the role's memory whether hosted in the widget or not. Today it does not: it shares a project key with the repo, and therefore with its own child.
- verification: a test spawns the same role under two different hosts with two different instance ids and asserts one resolved memory store and two distinct channel namespaces; a grep guard asserts no code path resolves a memory store for a role other than the caller's own.
- criticality: high
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-CWD-FORK] The invariant lives in ONE spawn path; the other never learned it

- premise: [INSIGHT-CWD-IS-THE-SCOPE-LEVER] records the fix as "baked into `IntelligenceHost.spawn` (project_dir -> child cwd) so it is never re-discovered per child." That is ONE spawn path. The Terminal Widget is a SECOND spawn path and it defaults `cwd=None`.
- key-insight: this is why the defect survived a fix. The invariant was not forgotten — it was implemented in one fork and the other fork was written without it, and the comment naming the hazard was sitting in the file the whole time (`ptycore.py:233`). [INSIGHT-FORK-DIVERGENCE-IS-A-CLASS] is the general form.
- requirement: the two spawn paths MUST de-fork onto ONE seam that derives cwd from the declared project dir. Neither path may accept a defaulted or inherited cwd for a managed instance.
- verification: a grep guard asserting `pty.fork(` is reachable from exactly one cwd-setting site, plus a CROSS-IMPLEMENTATION test that drives BOTH paths and asserts an identical resolved cwd for the same `(instance_id, role)`. A test that covers only one path re-certifies the fork.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-PROJECT-DIR] The project dir is keyed by ROLE — the reconciliation the builder MUST honour

- premise: David selected per-ROLE memory ([DD-FC-MEMORY-GRAIN]) AND a dedicated per-Intelligence home ([DD-FC-PROJECT-DIR-LAYOUT]). Those conflict as literally stated, because cwd can key only ONE thing: the project key derives from the absolute cwd and determines memory, transcripts and agent-def registration TOGETHER. A dir keyed by instance yields per-INSTANCE memory, which is not what was chosen.
- resolution: key the project dir by ROLE, not by instance id. `~/compose-instances/composeux-architect/` is the cwd for `architect` AND for `architect-hosted`; `~/compose-instances/composeio-hardware-architect/` is a different key. Instance identity is carried by `INSTANCE_ID` plus the manifest, NEVER by cwd — axis 2 of [CXC-FC-THREE-AXES], which is precisely the axis cwd must not carry.
- cwd-lever-scope: cwd is the lever for a TOP-LEVEL Intelligence ONLY. A dispatched subagent has an ephemeral worktree cwd and reaches the same store BY PATH, which is why [CXC-FC-STORE-ACCESS] is a requirement of this one and not an elaboration of it. A builder who implements only the cwd half has given the console's Intelligences memory and left every dispatched role cold.
- requirement: the manifest DECLARES the project dir so it is derived rather than remembered; the shared repo is attached as an ADDITIONAL WORKING DIRECTORY so file access is unaffected; a missing or defaulted role REFUSES rather than falling back to the repo.
- unaffected: transcripts co-locate but remain distinct files keyed by session uuid; session-pin resolution already globs across project dirs (`ptycore.py:418`); per-role agent-def registration is a bonus, because a role then sees its own agents.
- affected-shipped-code: ⚠ what DOES change is already-shipped code -- [CXC-FC-HOMES-REKEY].
- depends-on: [CXC-FC-STORE-ACCESS]
- criticality: foundational
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-HOMES-REKEY] `[CXC-IH-HOMES]` currently REFUSES what this spec ratifies — re-key it from instance to role

- premise: `intelligence/host/host.py::_enforce_home_isolation` (lines 202-227) enforces "one INSTANCE, one home" and raises `ValueError` when two DISTINCT ids resolve to the same project key: "home co-location: instance {id} resolves to project key {key}, already owned by {owner}." Its error message tells the operator to use an identity-keyed dir, `~/compose-instances/<id>/`.
- consequence: under [CXC-FC-PROJECT-DIR], `architect` and `architect-hosted` deliberately share `~/compose-instances/composeux-architect/`. The shipped guard would refuse the ratified configuration. This is not a conflict the narrative names; it was found by reading the code the decision lands on.
- requirement: `_enforce_home_isolation` MUST be re-keyed from INSTANCE to ROLE. The invariant becomes "one ROLE, one home; no two distinct ROLES may share a project key", and the ownership map `_home_by_key` maps project key -> ROLE. Its selftest (`host.py:355` onward) and both error message texts MUST be updated in the same change, and the suggested path in the message MUST become `~/compose-instances/<role>/` so the guidance does not point at the retired layout.
- prohibition: the guard MUST NOT simply be disabled to let the ratified layout through. A disabled guard is indistinguishable from an absent one, and the co-location it was written to catch — two distinct ROLES sharing a key — remains a real defect.
- verification: distinct roles sharing a home still raises; the same role's host and hosted child sharing a home does NOT raise; the deprecated `project_dir` alias keeps working (`host.py:106`).
- criticality: high
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-MEMORY-INDEX-DERIVED] Per-role memory REQUIRES a derived index; without it the build ships a concurrent-write defect

- premise: two live instances of one role now write one store at `~/compose-instances/<role>/memory/`, and under [CXC-FC-ROLE-MEMORY] a dispatched subagent of that role is a third concurrent writer. Memory is ONE FILE PER FACT, so real contention exists only on `MEMORY.md`, the index.
- requirement: the memory index MUST become DERIVABLE — regenerated by scanning `memory/*.md` frontmatter — rather than hand-maintained. Concurrent writers then touch disjoint files and the index is COMPUTED, so it cannot be corrupted by interleaving.
- rationale: this is the same define-once move used everywhere else in the platform, and it converts an accepted risk into a non-issue rather than documenting it as a caveat. A build that ships per-role memory WITHOUT the derived index has shipped the concurrent-write defect, and the failure is silent: an interleaved index loses a pointer, and a lost pointer in an automemory index is exactly the class nobody notices because the missing entry never surfaces.
- verification: a test writes two `memory/*.md` files concurrently and asserts the regenerated index contains BOTH; a second test asserts the index is byte-identical after two regenerations from the same inputs (idempotence); a guard asserts no code path hand-edits `MEMORY.md`.
- change: this is the mitigation that makes [DD-FC-MEMORY-GRAIN]'s accepted hazard acceptable, and it is a REQUIREMENT of that decision, not an optional companion to it.
- context_for_change:
  prior_state: "Memory's index was a hand-maintained `MEMORY.md` listing one line per memory file, written by whichever instance added the fact."
  reasoning: "Hand-maintenance is safe with one writer and corrupting with two. Per-role scoping makes two writers the NORMAL case rather than the exceptional one, so the mechanism has to change with the grain."
  rejected_alternative: "Locking `MEMORY.md`. Rejected: a lock serializes writers that have no reason to contend — they are adding disjoint facts — and a lock held by a crashed instance is a new failure mode with no owner."
  invalidating_assumption: "That memory remains one file per fact. If the store ever consolidates facts INTO the index, contention moves onto content and derivation alone stops being sufficient."
  implication: "The index is regenerable from the filesystem at any time, so a corrupted or deleted index is a recoverable state rather than a data loss."
- criticality: high
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-DEFORK] One seam, covered by a cross-implementation test

- requirement: the project-dir derivation MUST exist in exactly ONE function that both spawn paths call. Per [INSIGHT-FORK-DIVERGENCE-IS-A-CLASS], the invariant MUST be spec'd and covered by a cross-implementation test rather than patched in one place.
- verification: `project_dir_for(role)` (or equivalent) has exactly one definition site; a parametrised test drives `IntelligenceHost.spawn` and the Terminal Widget's spawn with the same `(instance_id, role)` and asserts identical resolved cwd, identical project key, and that the key is NOT the repo's.
- consequence: without this test the fix is a patch. The 2026-08-12 defect exists because one path was fixed and the fix was never made unforkable, so a per-path test would have passed on the fixed path and proved nothing about the other.
- depends-on: [CXC-FC-CWD-FORK]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

---

## 5. The pane is a two-mode uber-widget

### [CXC-FC-PANE-MODES] ENGAGE and CONFIGURE

- requirement: the collaboration uber-widget gains an engage/configure toggle (David's UX call, 2026-08-12). ENGAGE shows the collaboration elements — conversation thread, composer, link health, the Terminal Widget for THIS instance, its telemetry. CONFIGURE shows this agent's configuration widgets and current settings — its role manifest, its bootstrap set, its memory scope, notification rules, permission posture.
- default: a pane opens in ENGAGE. The toggle is per-pane state, never global: two panes may be in different modes simultaneously, because the reason to configure one Intelligence is rarely a reason to configure the other.
- requirement: a mode control MUST render its SELECTED state distinctly from its unselected state. Ratified 2026-08-12 after adversarial QA: without this clause the toggle's affordance is UNFALSIFIABLE — a build can satisfy every other word of this node with two identical labels, and did. The first implementation bound the chips' `background` to a mode expression and the binding was DISCARDED IN SILENCE (`background` was absent from BINDABLE_ATTRS), so the chips rendered as bare identical text, the scene parsed clean and every test passed.
- verification: machine — a render test asserts the selected and unselected controls differ in a rendered attribute, not merely that a mode namespace is declared. Glass — David can tell which mode a pane is in WITHOUT clicking.
- see-also: [CXC-FC-BIND-NO-SILENT-DROP]
- constraint: both modes bind from the SAME `(instance_id, role)` params. The configure mode reads `intelligence/<id>/config/*`, which is already per-id today.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-BIND-NO-SILENT-DROP] A binding the engine cannot apply must REFUSE, never fall through

- premise: (found by adversarial QA, 2026-08-12) `background: {expr: ...}` on the pane's mode chips resolved to NOTHING. `background` was not in `bindings.py::BINDABLE_ATTRS`, so `extract_bindings` skipped the key, the dict then failed the colour parse and became `None`, and no warning was emitted anywhere. The scene parsed clean, the suite was green, and the only available evidence was a human noticing an affordance that could not be selected.
- requirement: an attribute carrying a binding site (`expr:` / `namespace:`) that the engine cannot bind MUST raise at parse time, naming the attribute and what IS bindable. Attributes whose binding-shaped dict is consumed by a DIFFERENT mechanism MUST be enumerated in an explicit registry naming each consumer, so that an unregistered attribute is the loud case.
- rationale: a silently dropped binding is this platform's recurring failure class and it is worse than a crash, because the surface still renders — it renders a state the system is not in. A refusal converts an invisible authoring error into a load-time stop.
- prohibition: the registry MUST NOT be a pattern match over "looks handled". A regex would re-open the silence it closes; each exemption names its consumer or it is not an exemption.
- criticality: high
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-SPLIT-RULE] The splitting rule, which is also the design test

- rule: any dial whose correct value could DIFFER between two Intelligences belongs to the PANE; anything about the console itself, or shared by all Intelligences, stays in the console's CONFIG TAB.
- rationale: this is not a cosmetic reorganisation. Today's tab mixes agent-specific and console dials BECAUSE there has only ever been one agent, so the distinction cost nothing. The rule maps exactly onto [CXC-FC-TIERS] — the tab holds {console-self + PLATFORM}, the pane holds {ROLE + INSTANCE}.
- application: a builder unsure where a dial belongs applies the rule literally by naming a second Intelligence and asking whether the value could differ. If it could, it is a pane dial.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-CONFIG-TAB] The console config tab DE-SCOPES

- requirement: the existing Intelligence Console configuration tab MUST de-scope to exactly two things. CONSOLE-SELF: heartbeat, retention, appearance, agents, skills, which panes are visible. PLATFORM-tier bootstrap: dusk gifts, collaboration and design principles, the wake ritual.
- requirement: every remaining dial MUST move to the pane. `intelligence/<id>/config/*` is ALREADY per-id — it is the TAB that assumes one, so this is a re-binding of existing surfaces, not new persistence.
- verification: no widget in the config tab binds a topic containing a literal instance id; every pane-tier dial round-trips through `intelligence/<id>/config/*` for the pane's own id.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

---

## 6. The four data-layer singletons

### [CXC-FC-S1-BRIDGES] N channel bridges from the declared instance list

- premise: `data_sources.py::build_console_sources` appends exactly one `channel_bridge` (line 508) steered by a single `chat_id`. This is why, when the console's single bridge was pointed at the hosted child on 2026-08-12, the HOST lost its bus voice entirely — not a pane change, a TRANSPORT change.
- requirement: the console MUST start ONE bridge per declared instance, each with `--id <instance_id>`.
- evidence: the mechanism is already correct -- the flock is keyed `channel-bridge-<id>` (`channel_bridge.py:192-212`, lock file `composeio-channel-bridge-<id>.lock`), and the client id is the same stable non-PID string. Distinct ids provably cannot contend; the mechanism was simply never instantiated more than once.
- verification: with two instances declared, two bridges run, two distinct lock files exist, both `intelligence/<id>/out` streams reach their panes, and neither instance's channel goes quiet when the other is added.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-S2-TELEMETRY] N telemetry publishers, and a transcript pin that can be RE-resolved

- premise: one `self_publisher` / `transcript_publisher` / `deliverable_monitor` set exists (`data_sources.py:484-495`), all pinned to a single `intelligence_id`, and `resolve_session_jsonl` (line 152) runs ONCE, at console boot, for one session.
- requirement: the telemetry set MUST become per-instance, and the transcript pin MUST become RE-RESOLVABLE at runtime rather than boot-once.
- rationale-staleness-twin: the second half of the requirement -- a re-host leaves a long-running console pinned to a dead transcript. Observed: a console up 3 days pinned to a transcript dead for 2, rendering perfectly with every gauge frozen. A frozen gauge that renders is worse than a missing one, because the surface reports a state the system is not in.
- requirement: a pin that has gone stale MUST be visible on the glass, not merely re-resolved silently. Re-resolution fixes the data; the visible marker is what prevents the same class recurring unnoticed when re-resolution itself fails.
- verification: two instances publish to disjoint `self/<id>/*` and `thread/<id>/*` namespaces; a test kills and re-hosts one instance and asserts the console re-pins to the NEW transcript within a bounded interval and that the stale interval was visible.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-S3-ROSTER] ONE roster source — a single computation, never per-pane discovery

- requirement: exactly ONE subscriber to `intelligence/+/channel/alive` applies a liveness timeout and publishes `fleet/roster` = `[{id, role, pid, instance, last_seen, hosted}]`.
- prohibition: if each pane did its own wildcard discovery that is fork-divergence BY CONSTRUCTION — N implementations of one liveness rule, guaranteed to disagree under load. One computation, one topic, N readers.
- key-insight: the heartbeat already carries `instance`, the session uuid, which is the key to that agent's transcript (`intelligence/channel/channel.mjs:243-245` publishes `{ts, id, pid, start, instance, delivery, unacked}`). So HOSTED CHILDREN AND INDEPENDENTLY-LAUNCHED PEERS BECOME THE SAME CODE PATH — the console need not have spawned something in order to instrument it.
- see-also: [CXC-FC-HOST-AS-PANE]
- constraint: (measured) `role` and `hosted` are NOT on the heartbeat. The roster source MUST resolve `role` through the manifest chain the id already resolves through and MUST derive `hosted` from the host's own roster; when either is unresolvable it MUST publish an explicit `unknown` rather than a guess, because a guessed role keys nothing and a guessed `hosted` flag mislabels a peer as a child.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-S4-BBOX] `_app/console/bbox` is a hardcoded constant; it becomes `_app/<app_id>/bbox`

- premise: (verified) `python/composeio/render/engine/runtime.py:79` reads `CONSOLE_BBOX_NS = "_app/console/bbox"`. Every scene the engine launches publishes its geometry to a topic named `console`. Measured 2026-08-12: FOUR apps clobbering one topic at 2 Hz, so [DEC-LAUNCH-PLACEMENT]'s consumer reads whichever published last.
- requirement: the namespace MUST become `_app/<app_id>/bbox`, derived from the running app's id.
- note: this is why it is a small change -- the runtime ALREADY reads an override, `COMPOSEIO_CONSOLE_BBOX_NS` (`runtime.py:315`), and already gates publication on a launched-child sentinel (`runtime.py:317`). The seam exists; only the DEFAULT is a constant. The fix is to derive the default, not to build a new mechanism.
- verification: two apps running simultaneously publish to two distinct topics; a placement consumer subscribed to one app's bbox is unaffected by the other's publish rate; the string `_app/console/bbox` appears nowhere as a hardcoded default.
- dangling-reference-note: [DEC-LAUNCH-PLACEMENT] likewise has NO node in the decision graph — it is cited at `runtime.py:64`, `runtime.py:308` and once in [SPEC:CXC-TERM], where it already dangles. The edge is authored so the second citation does not make the gap look smaller than it is.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-HOST-AS-PANE] The console's own Intelligence is an ORDINARY roster entry

- requirement: the console's own Intelligence MUST become an ordinary roster entry rather than the special case that owns `self/*`. No pane, no data source and no widget may branch on "is this the host".
- rationale: this is what actually CLOSES [INSIGHT-SELF-IS-NOT-YET-A-PEER] — not "the console shows N others" but "the console has no privileged self". A privileged self is a second code path for one concept, and it is the path that produced the chimera David observed on 2026-08-12: the chat pane showing the CHILD while every gauge showed the HOST, with nothing on the glass saying which was which.
- verification: a grep guard that no data source or widget special-cases the host id; the console's own pane is instantiated by the same `{use: intelligence_pane, ...}` line as every other.
- see-also: [CXC-FC-IDENTITY-ON-GLASS]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [CXC-FC-IDENTITY-ON-GLASS] Every pane names its instance and role VISIBLY — a hard requirement

- requirement: every pane MUST display its `instance_id` and its `role` visibly at all times. This is not polish and MUST NOT be deferred to a later slice.
- rationale: a surface that renders two Intelligences without distinguishing them is the report-a-state-the-system-is-not-in failure in UX form. The 2026-08-12 chimera is the worked example — every element rendered correctly and the composition was a lie.
- verification: machine — a render test asserts both strings are present and non-empty in every pane's capture. Glass — David looks at a two-pane console and can say which Intelligence he is talking to WITHOUT clicking anything. The human gate is mandatory and non-delegable, because a test asserting a string is present cannot see that it is illegible, truncated, or attached to the wrong pane.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

---

## 7. Ordering

### [CXC-FC-ORDER] The author's ordering — NOT a delivery contract

- constraint: under [PRINCIPLE-ONE-PASS] this ships in ONE pass if the spec is complete. Slice numbering is the author's ordering, never a delivery contract, and a builder who ships slice 1 and stops has shipped a console that renders two panes over one memory store.
- ordering: (1) `instance_id` to widget/param scope plus the `intelligence_pane` composite — PROVE WITH TWO INSTANCES IN ONE SCENE; (2) per-instance cwd/project dir and the de-fork of both spawn paths; (3) N channel bridges from declared instances; (4) roster source plus per-instance telemetry publishers; (5) host-as-a-pane and `_app/<app_id>/bbox`; (6) the engage/configure toggle and the config-tab de-scope; (7) discovery drives pane visibility — [CAP-BINDABLE-ORDER] is BUILT AND TESTED (9 passing tests), so this is a binding, not new layout code.
- note: item 7 is the ADDITIVE step explicitly outside this build's acceptance ([DD-FC-PANE-SOURCE]). It is listed because knowing the next step is what keeps step 1 from being built in a way that forecloses it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

---

## 8. Decision points — ALL RATIFIED 2026-08-12, all CLOSED

### [DD-FC-MEMORY-GRAIN] Memory is scoped PER ROLE

- decision: is a memory store scoped per ROLE (all instances of one agent def share it) or per INSTANCE (each live instance has its own)?
- chosen: PER ROLE. All instances of one role share a memory store; a respawned child inherits the role's accumulated pointers.
- sharpened-same-day: "role" means EVERY role in the roster, not only top-level Intelligences, and a role's name is the BASENAME OF ITS ROLE DESCRIPTOR so the store set is derived rather than maintained ([CXC-FC-ROLE-MEMORY]). The mechanism is ONE store per role with two access paths, because cwd is unavailable to a worktree-dispatched subagent ([CXC-FC-STORE-ACCESS]).
- alternatives: per INSTANCE — the architect's lean, argued from [PRINCIPLE-STRUCTURE-SURVIVES-THE-BOUNDARY]'s memory-rule note that automemory "is specific to THIS instance, whereas every other home is shared to disk".
- rationale: David's call, 2026-08-12, taken against the architect's lean. A respawned child that inherits nothing re-learns the same pointers every spawn, which is the cost per-instance scoping actually charges. The mechanism is identical either way (a cwd per unit); only the granularity differs.
- invalidating_assumption: that memory remains one file per fact with a derivable index. If contention ever moves onto content rather than the index, the shared store stops being safe and the grain reopens.
- consequence: the accepted hazard is two live writers on one store, and [CXC-FC-MEMORY-INDEX-DERIVED] is its REQUIRED mitigation, not an optional companion.
- status: decided
- lifecycle-position: decided
- decided-in-session: 2026-08-12
- recoverability: PREFERENCE
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [DD-FC-PANE-SOURCE] Panes are DECLARED, not discovered

- decision: does the scene DECLARE which Intelligences get panes, or does the roster DISCOVER them and drive pane visibility?
- chosen: DECLARED. The scene names which Intelligences get panes.
- alternatives: roster-driven discovery, which [CAP-BINDABLE-ORDER] already makes mechanically possible.
- rationale: deterministic, testable, goldens stable, no surprise windows. Roster-driven discovery is an ADDITIVE second step and is explicitly NOT in this build's acceptance.
- invalidating_assumption: that the declared set stays small enough to maintain by hand. A fleet large enough that declaring panes is clerical work reopens this in favour of discovery.
- consequence: acceptance criterion FC-C1 is a TWO-INSTANCE proof from a declared scene; nothing in this build may depend on discovery having landed.
- status: decided
- lifecycle-position: decided
- decided-in-session: 2026-08-12
- recoverability: PREFERENCE
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

### [DD-FC-PROJECT-DIR-LAYOUT] `~/compose-instances/<role>/`, outside the repo

- decision: where does an Intelligence's project dir live, and what is it keyed by?
- chosen: `~/compose-instances/<role>/`, OUTSIDE the repo, with the shared repo attached as an additional working directory.
- alternatives: (a) the repo itself — the current accidental state, which collapses every Intelligence into one project key; (b) `~/compose-instances/<id>/`, the per-INSTANCE layout the shipped `[CXC-IH-HOMES]` guard suggests today.
- rationale: David's call, 2026-08-12. Outside the repo so the project key is stable under checkout moves and so a fleet does not pollute the working tree. Keyed by ROLE because cwd keys memory, transcripts and agent-def registration TOGETHER, and per-role memory was chosen — see the reconciliation at [CXC-FC-PROJECT-DIR].
- invalidating_assumption: that the harness continues to key the project dir off the absolute cwd. If the harness ever exposed a direct project-key or memory-scope setting, the cwd would stop being the lever and instance identity could be re-separated from role.
- consequence: option (b) is not merely rejected — it is the CURRENTLY SHIPPED guard, so this decision forces [CXC-FC-HOMES-REKEY].
- status: decided
- lifecycle-position: decided
- decided-in-session: 2026-08-12
- recoverability: PREFERENCE
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLEET]

